A new hand-run workflow, **Facebook Reels**, that does for the queue's Reels what **Facebook link posts** does for link posts. Instagram stays manual (no API scheduling).

**How it schedules** (Meta's Reels Publishing API, read 9 Nov 2026):
1. download the video from the Drive folder, checked against the size Drive lists;
2. `POST /{page}/video_reels` with `upload_phase=start`;
3. send the bytes to the returned `upload_url`; the token is refused for any host but `rupload.facebook.com`;
4. `upload_phase=finish`, `video_state=SCHEDULED`, `scheduled_publish_time`, `description` = the caption;
5. read back until Facebook reports `publish_status: scheduled` at exactly that time; anything else is a PROBLEM and the run fails.

**What stops a second Reel** (pure, in `src/facebook/reels.ts`): the Page already holds a scheduled Reel for that minute, or a scheduled or published Reel whose description has the same first line (a Reel scheduled by hand in the Planner, perhaps at another time or with an edited caption). Drafts and errored Reels don't count. The window is the same 27 days as link posts (Meta's guide says 29; the link-post guide's 30 was not what Facebook enforced).

**Before the first `schedule` run, a dry run is the control.** It lists the time of every Reel the Page has scheduled. The Reels scheduled by hand in the Planner (S3 16 Nov, F8 18 Nov, S4 23 Nov, F10 25 Nov, S5 30 Nov, all 19:30) must appear there; if they don't, the edge can't see Planner Reels, the duplicate check is blind, and this must not schedule anything.

Checks: `npm test` 33 passed (10 new in `test/reels.test.ts`). Mutations, each restored: no same-time check fails 2; no same-first-line check fails 1; drafts counting as done fails 1; no window fails 1; video not checked fails 1. No typecheck exists in this repo (`node` strips types), so the new script was not compiled beyond running the tests that import its rules.

