O1 of `docs/roadmap/OCEAN-AND-WEATHER-ROADMAP.md` (Phase 0, W0.1–W0.2). Each later phase reads this to know which body of water a point is in and what its level is: depth damping, the shore layer, fetch, wake masks and the physics fields. It applies to the sea and also to lakes and ponds.

## What it adds

**`cvc/world/water_bodies.h`** (standard library only, deterministic):
- **Labelling:** `build_water_bodies(ground, grid, params)` labels the 8-connected runs of samples whose ground is below the water level.
  - Every run that reaches the grid's edge is part of the one **sea**. Those inlets meet past the world; a standard terrain_lab world has about ten of them.
  - Each enclosed run is a **lake**, or a **pond** under `pond_max_area_m2` (1 ha, decision D7).
  - `stream` is reserved until hydrology (W1) gives each depression its own level.
- **Per-sample planes:** `body`, `level`, `depth`, and the shore grid:
  - `shore_m`: the signed distance to the shore, + offshore. It is an exact Euclidean distance transform, so it is within half a sample of the waterline.
  - `normal_x` / `normal_y`: the unit shore normal, pointing offshore. It can be smoothed over `normal_smooth_m` so crests don't twist on the medial axis.
  - `slope`: tan β, for the Iribarren number later.
- **Per-body statistics:** level, area, bounds, deepest point, and `fetch_m`, the major axis of the body's second-moment ellipse, for the fetch-limited wind sea on lakes.
- **Helpers:** queries (`body_at`, bilinear `sample`), `water_bodies_summary` and `water_bodies_debug_rgb`.

**The distance transform is shared now, in a new module, `cvc::algo`.** `cvc::world` may not include `cvc::nav` (a configure-time rule), so the transform moved out of nav into `cvc/algo/distance_transform.h`, where both can use it:
- nav's binary EDT moved unchanged from `grid_nav.cpp`. It is still Felzenszwalb–Huttenlocher, bit-identical to GRL-SNAM's `sdf_nav._edt2`, with its exact integer-sweep fast path.
- It offers `edt2_squared`, `edt2_squared_both` (a mask and its complement in one pass) and `edt2_squared_rows` (a row at a time, for `build_sdf`).
- `cvc::algo` depends on the standard library only. A configure-time check fails the build if an algo source includes another libcvc library.
- `nav::edt2_squared` and `nav::build_sdf` call it, and `build_sdf` keeps its own per-thread rows.
- `water_bodies` uses `edt2_squared_both`. The transform works in cells, so `build_water_bodies` now requires a square grid and throws on any other; every caller's grid is square.

**`generated_world::water`** is built in a new `world_builder` stage, `shore`, after `water` and before `lod`:
- It reads the terrain mesh's own samples with the roads graded in, so its shore is the one the mesh draws.
- It takes every `water_bodies_stride(n)`-th sample to stay at most 1025 a side.
- It is one step, like the other single-call stages. The progress weights are rebalanced (mesh 0.24 → 0.22, water 0.08 → 0.07, shore 0.03).

**terrain_lab.ari debug view:**
- The world panel's **Colour by** gains **Water bodies**: the sea blue, lakes teal, ponds green with a shade per body, dry land grey, and a contour every 25 m of shore distance.
- In that mode the water surfaces' texture (`surface_painter::take_water`) takes the same colours, so the bodies show where the water covers the ground.
- The realizer's first paint now goes through `surface_painter` too, so a document opened in this mode starts right.
- The panel shows a **water** line (`world.stats.water`, e.g. "1 sea, 36 lakes, 203 ponds").
- `color: water` works in a document. Both the cvcGL realizer and the headless host accept it.
- Checked with `arigl snapshot --set world.color=4` on terrain_lab.ari:
  - the standard 1.2 km world shows its edge inlets as one blue sea, the large central lake teal, and the ponds green;
  - the island preset shows the sea's contours following the coast.

